  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nToday through Tonight...\n\nBroad cyclonic still in place from a departing low pressure system \nalong with lingering moisture continues to promote isolated to \nscattered light showers across portions of central Indiana. Look for \nthese showers to diminish in coverage through this evening as the \ndisturbance shifts southeastward. Thunderstorms are not expected due \nto very limited instability. \n\nSurface high pressure building in behind the departing system will \nhelp clear out clouds this evening into tonight. Meanwhile, the \npressure gradient relaxing should allow winds to become light. \nExpect patchy fog to develop late overnight once winds become light \nas the ground remains saturated from recent rainfall. Any fog that \ndevelops will mix out quickly Monday morning. \n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nExpect mostly quiet weather conditions given surface high pressure \nremains in control across the region. Latest guidance now suggest \nthe departing low pressure system today will not retrograde back \ninto central Indiana after becoming cutoff. The forecast is now \ncompletely dry for Tuesday and Wednesday for this reason. That said, \ndeeper moisture advecting back near far southeast portions of \ncentral Indiana along with weak dynamics from the system and diurnal \nheating could support a stray shower. \n\nWednesday night onward...\n\nMultiple embedded shortwaves traversing the region Wednesday night \nthrough end of the week will promote additional convective chances. \nExact details remain uncertain at this time due to diverging model \nsolutions. The greatest precipitation chances are on Thursday when \nthe strongest forcing overlaps with deeper moisture surging \nnorthward. Thunderstorms will be possible at times as guidance \ndepicts sufficient destabilization. Severe weather is not expected \nat this time, but some guidance depicting moderate destabilization \nand modest enhancement of mid-upper level flow from passing waves \nsuggest a low threat for severe thunderstorms is not out of the \nquestion. Model trends will continue to be monitored closely.",
